        "The condition P(0) = 0 forces C = 0, so P(x) = x^4/4 - x^2/2.",
        "Verification: d/dx(x^4/4 - x^2/2) = x^3 - x, and P(0) = 0 - 0 = 0."
      ],
      "status": "proved"
    }
  ],
  "proofs": [
    {
      "claim_id": "P-integral-x3-x",
      "argument": "Uniqueness: if P1 and P2 both satisfy the two conditions, then (P1 - P2)' = 0, so P1 - P2 is a constant polynomial; evaluating at 0 gives P1(0) - P2(0) = 0, hence P1 = P2. Existence: term-by-term antidifferentiation of x^3 - x in Q[x] yields x^4/4 - x^2/2 + C; P(0) = 0 pins C = 0. Direct check: (x^4/4 - x^2/2)' = x^3 - x and x^4/4 - x^2/2 evaluated at 0 is 0. Therefore the unique answer is P(x) = x^4/4 - x^2/2.",
      "assumptions": [
        "Standard formal derivative on Q[x]: d/dx(x^n) = n·x^(n-1), extended linearly.",
        "A polynomial with identically zero derivative is constant (valid in characteristic 0, in particular over Q)."
      ],

