{
  "claim": "There is a unique polynomial P ∈ Q[x] with P(0)=0 and P'(x)=x³−x, namely P(x) = x⁴/4 − x²/2, whose coefficient list in ascending powers is [\"0\", \"0\", \"-1/2\", \"0\", \"1/4\"].",
  "assumptions_or_dependencies": [
    "Standard formal derivative on Q[x]: (x^n)' = n·x^(n−1), linearity, and (constant)' = 0.",
    "Antidifferentiation in Q[x] is unique up to an additive constant; the condition P(0)=0 fixes that constant.",
    "Rational coefficients are permitted and encoded as strings \"p/q\" in lowest terms with q>0."
  ],
  "derivation": [
    "Antiderivative term by term: an antiderivative of x³ is x⁴/4, since (x⁴/4)' = 4x³/4 = x³.",
    "An antiderivative of −x is −x²/2, since (−x²/2)' = −2x/2 = −x.",
    "Hence every polynomial with P'(x) = x³ − x has the form P(x) = x⁴/4 − x²/2 + C for some constant C ∈ Q.",
    "The condition P(0)=0 gives 0 = 0⁴/4 − 0²/2 + C = C, so C = 0.",
    "Therefore P(x) = x⁴/4 − x²/2 = 0 + 0·x − (1/2)x² + 0·x³ + (1/4)x⁴.",
    "Verification: P'(x) = 2·(−1/2)x + 4·(1/4)x³ = −x + x³ = x³ − x, and P(0) = 0. Both requirements hold."
  ],
  "certificate": {
    "coefficients": ["0", "0", "-1/2", "0", "1/4"]
  }
}

